WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.692 --> 00:00:11.879
The departed at 6:30 p.m.

2
00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:14.821
on November 7 and steamed toward Yokohama, Japan.

3
00:00:15.622 --> 00:00:21.066
The ship was completely booked, and only two staterooms in the rear were unoccupied, those reserved for Mr.

4
00:00:21.086 --> 00:00:22.087
Fogg and Aouda.

5
00:00:23.548 --> 00:00:28.891
Shortly after Fix had left Passepartout in the opium den, two waiters lifted him to the bed reserved

6
00:00:28.931 --> 00:00:29.572
for smokers.

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.543
Three hours later he woke up and fought off the hypnotic influence of the drug.

8
00:00:35.905 --> 00:00:40.188
He hurried to the steamer and, when he reached his room onboard, immediately passed out.

9
00:00:41.209 --> 00:00:45.853
When Passepartout woke up the next morning, he staggered onto the deck, wondering what Mr.

10
00:00:45.873 --> 00:00:46.574
Fogg would say.

11
00:00:46.594 --> 00:00:50.477
"At least I didn't miss the steamer, which is the most important thing!"

12
00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:53.860
he said to himself, as he sipped some strong black coffee.

13
00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:58.624
Passepartout suddenly thought of Fix and how the man had left him in the tavern.

14
00:00:59.664 --> 00:01:02.965
"As for that rascal, I hope we are rid of him," he said.

15
00:01:02.985 --> 00:01:05.106
"A detective on the track of Mr.

16
00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:08.608
Fogg, accusing my master of robbing the Bank of England—ridiculous!

17
00:01:09.208 --> 00:01:10.569
Mr. Fogg is not a robber."

18
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.651
Passepartout finished his breakfast and then descended to the staterooms.

19
00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:20.433
Mr. Fogg and Aouda were nowhere to be found!

20
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:23.114
There was no luggage nothing.

21
00:01:23.794 --> 00:01:26.075
Passepartout wondered if he was even on the right ship.

22
00:01:27.015 --> 00:01:29.825
He rushed to the purser and asked, "Am I on the Carnatic?"

23
00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:34.547
"Yes, on the way to Yokohama," replied the purser, a little surprised.

24
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.648
"May I look at the passenger list?"

25
00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:37.989
asked Passepartout.

26
00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:39.450
"Of course."

27
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.752
The purser had the list with him and showed Passepartout.

28
00:01:43.672 --> 00:01:46.994
When he had read every name, Passepartout fell thunderstruck on a seat.

29
00:01:48.195 --> 00:01:50.616
Mr. Fogg and Aouda were not on the passenger list!

30
00:01:51.476 --> 00:01:52.937
Now he realized what had happened.

31
00:01:53.938 --> 00:01:57.120
The time of departure had changed, and he had been unable to tell Mr.

32
00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.860
Fogg. Mr.

33
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.501
Fogg and Aouda had missed the steamer!

34
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.722
Passepartout felt sick to his stomach with rage.

35
00:02:04.563 --> 00:02:08.424
He tore at his hair and vowed that, if he ever saw Fix again, he would even the

36
00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:09.465
score with him indeed!

37
00:02:11.345 --> 00:02:14.907
After his anger calmed, Passepartout thought through his situation.

38
00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:17.428
It was certainly not an attractive one.

39
00:02:18.408 --> 00:02:20.249
He was on the way to Japan with no money.

40
00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:22.690
And what was he going to do when he got there?

41
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.192
At dawn on November 13, the Carnatic reached the port of Yokohama.

42
00:02:28.384 --> 00:02:29.605
Passepartout went ashore.

43
00:02:30.286 --> 00:02:32.968
He had nothing better to do than wander aimlessly through the city.

44
00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:40.034
In the crowded streets, he saw all sorts of people: priests in white robes, police officers with black

45
00:02:40.094 --> 00:02:45.559
pointed hats, sailors in blue cotton, and royal guards in stiff armor with long thin swords on their

46
00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:53.626
backs. He also saw beautiful groves, tall sacred gates, bridges hidden in the midst of bamboo, and temples

47
00:02:53.686 --> 00:02:55.328
shaded by immense cedar trees.

48
00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.801
By the next morning, hunger gnawed at Passepartout's empty stomach.

49
00:03:00.641 --> 00:03:04.942
With no money, he thought of selling his precious family watch but changed his mind.

50
00:03:05.803 --> 00:03:07.183
He would rather starve first!

51
00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:12.324
He decided he could earn some money as a vagabond performer but then thought he was too well

52
00:03:12.384 --> 00:03:13.685
dressed to be believable.

53
00:03:14.985 --> 00:03:18.086
After a long search, he found a small shop that bought old clothes.

54
00:03:19.206 --> 00:03:23.767
He asked the owner if he could exchange his European clothes for an old Japanese coat and a

55
00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:25.288
faded turban he saw in the window.

56
00:03:26.136 --> 00:03:28.818
The owner also gave him a few pieces of silver in the trade.

57
00:03:29.859 --> 00:03:33.981
Passepartout immediately found a food stand and ate what little breakfast he could afford.

58
00:03:34.001 --> 00:03:39.045
"Now," he thought to himself, "I mustn't lose my head.

59
00:03:39.205 --> 00:03:41.126
I must consider how I am going to feed myself."

60
00:03:42.047 --> 00:03:47.470
He walked toward the docks, having decided that passengers from visiting steamers would be the most generous customers.

61
00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:51.413
On the way he was surprised to see a sign in English.

62
00:03:52.574 --> 00:03:55.556
It read: Japanese Acrobats The Long Noses!

63
00:03:56.564 --> 00:03:59.085
Come and see them before their departure to the United States!

64
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:00.726
Grand Attraction!

65
00:04:02.027 --> 00:04:03.228
Passepartout was excited.

66
00:04:03.788 --> 00:04:05.249
He had been an acrobat in his youth.

67
00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:10.672
If he was hired, he could find himself on the way to the United States, exactly where Mr.

68
00:04:10.692 --> 00:04:11.332
Fogg was headed!

69
00:04:12.092 --> 00:04:16.014
A quarter of an hour later, Passepartout stopped before a large, colorful tent.

70
00:04:16.835 --> 00:04:18.696
He entered and asked for the owner of the show.

71
00:04:20.337 --> 00:04:23.939
Mr. Batulcar appeared immediately and interviewed Passepartout.

72
00:04:23.959 --> 00:04:26.120
"You are pretty strong, eh?"

73
00:04:26.544 --> 00:04:27.025
asked Mr.

74
00:04:27.065 --> 00:04:29.727
Batulcar. "Especially after a good meal!"

75
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.328
responded Passepartout.

76
00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:33.050
"And can you sing?"

77
00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:34.651
Mr. Batulcar asked.

78
00:04:34.671 --> 00:04:36.993
"Yes," said Passepartout.

79
00:04:37.013 --> 00:04:42.998
"But can you sing standing on your head, with a top spinning on your left foot and a

80
00:04:43.118 --> 00:04:44.760
sword balanced on your right?"

81
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:45.761
asked Mr.

82
00:04:45.781 --> 00:04:50.945
Batulcar. "I think so," said Passepartout, and he proceeded to demonstrate his skill.

83
00:04:50.965 --> 00:04:52.466
"You are hired!"

84
00:04:52.807 --> 00:04:53.407
exclaimed Mr.

85
00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.245
Batulcar. Well, what a day!

86
00:04:59.885 --> 00:05:04.047
Wandering the streets of Yokohama with no money and then finding myself hired as an acrobat.

87
00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:07.989
Yokohama certainly is a unique place, where East meets West!

88
00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:15.814
In Japan the Meiji period ("Meiji" means "enlightened rule") began in 1868, and so far it has brought

89
00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:16.934
many changes to Japan.

90
00:05:17.635 --> 00:05:22.437
Leaders have decided to model Japan's government, legal system, military, and economy on the West.

91
00:05:23.118 --> 00:05:27.100
And according to a new policy, "knowledge shall be sought throughout the world."

92
00:05:28.188 --> 00:05:34.230
So Japan has employed over three-thousand foreign experts in fields such as science, engineering, and English language instruction

93
00:05:34.431 --> 00:05:35.771
to help modernize society.

94
00:05:36.431 --> 00:05:40.793
The government is also sending many Japanese students overseas to Europe and America to study.

95
00:05:41.493 --> 00:05:43.894
These changes will surely transform the people of Japan.

96
00:05:44.694 --> 00:05:46.895
Perhaps some of them will come to see my acrobat performance!
